                <pb facs="1126" n="1026" sameAs="frus:frus1880/pg1026"/>

                <head>No. 654.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Fish to Mr. Hunter.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Consulate</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Tunis</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1879-07-08"><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 8, 1879</date>.
                        (Received July 26.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 44.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inform the Secretary of
                    State that on the 4th instant the United States flag was displayed at this
                    consulate, and that at each of the foreign consulates in this city the flags of
                    the respective governments were displayed in commemoration of the one hundred
                    and third anniversary of American Independence. During the day the
                    representatives of foreign governments residing here, presented their
                    congratulations. At 10 o’clock a.m., the Bey sent General Si Haïda, governor of
                    El Arad; General Si Mohammed Rechid, attached to the new ministry; and Mr.
                    Gabriel Valensi, interpreter, to present His Highnesses congratulations and
                    assurances of continued amity towards the government of the United States. And
                    he especially desired that his compliments and assurances of friendship and good
                    will be presented to the President.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>GEO. W. FISH,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">United States Consul,
                            Tunis.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
